//@ run-pass
//@ requires: madaros
//@ exit-code: 1
//@ description: sed_ker_lz_gen hard-refuses out-of-range k (fail-closed via process_exit(1)); the fabricated zero is dead
// Full Test Suite CI uses souc-stage2 (lean_single). Madaros runs this graph.
// Requires a Madaros with POSIX-extern execution (#1755): process_exit -> exit(1).
//
// NEGATIVE / fail-closed witness. Before hardening, sed_ker_lz_gen(k) left the
// output all-zero for any k∉[0,3]; z*0 = 0, so that zero vector passed every
// kernel-membership check while being no generator at all — a fabricated zero.
//
// Now an out-of-range index is a hard refusal: the constructor calls
// process_exit(1) and never returns a value. This test is the proof:
//
//   - If the guard fires (correct), the process exits 1 and the harness sees
//     exit-code 1 == expected  → PASS.
//   - If the guard ever regressed and the call returned a (fabricated) vector,
//     control would fall through to `return 0`, the harness would see exit 0
//     != 1  → FAIL.
//
// So this file passes IFF the fabricated-zero path is genuinely gone.

use algebra::sedenion_kernel::{sed_ker_lz_gen}

fn main() -> i64 with IO, Mut, Div, Panic {
    var g: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    // k = 99 is out of [0,3]. Must abort with exit 1, not return a fake basis
    // element. process_exit(1) has return type `!`, so nothing after it runs
    // when the guard is hit.
    sed_ker_lz_gen(99, &!g)
    // Reached only if the refusal regressed. Exit 0 here so the expected
    // exit-code:1 assertion fails loudly — that is the point of this test.
    0
}
